  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through Wednesday)\nIssued at 217 PM MDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nHigher moisture will be in place along and east of a backdoor front \nacross far western NM Saturday with PWATS around 1 to 1.3 inches \nalong and east of the Rio Grande Valley. Showers and storms will get \ngoing across central and southern mountain ranges midday before \nmoving south into surrounding lower elevations during the afternoon \nand evening hours due to upper level northerly flow on the western \nperiphery of a 598 to 599 upper high over Arizona. Storms along and \neast of the Continental Divide will be capable of producing damaging \nwind gusts and large hail due to bulk shear values of 30 to 40 kts \nalong with locally heavy rainfall due to the high moisture content \nmentioned above. The flash flooding risk will be highest on recent \nburn scars (i.e. Ruidoso area). Elsewhere, the heat will be the main \nstory across northwest and far west central NM with high \ntemperatures in the upper 90s to around 100 due to being close to \nthe center of the upper high. Heat Advisories will likely be needed \nfor the Northwest Plateau and well as the West Central Plateau \n(including Gallup and Zuni) due to recovery operations from flash \nflooding events the last few weeks. Storms Saturday evening will be \nfocused along and south of I-40 before tapering off across southern \nNM overnight. \n\nThe upper high will begin nosing back east to over the Four Corners \narea Sunday before flattening across much of the desert SW Monday \nthrough Wednesday. This will result in a lower storm coverage with \nany isolated activity limited to the mountains Sunday and Monday due \nto lower PWATS around 0.6 to 0.9 inches. With the 595 to 596 upper \nhigh overhead, the heat will reintensify across central and eastern \nNM with upper 90s to low 100s common across lower elevations. \nEventual heat Advisories across northwest and far west central NM \nthis weekend will potentially need to be expanded to some central \nand eastern zones come Monday. PWATS gradually increase Tuesday and \nWednesday to around 1 to 1.1 inches helping to result in a slightly \nhigher coverage of showers and thunderstorms across the western and \ncentral high terrain and nearby lower elevations.",
